  "measures": [],
  "notes": "A 300 nm silica sphere at the focus of an NA 0.8 objective (an ideal lens, f = 2 mm), in 100 mW of 1550 nm light polarised in the bench plane. Select the screen at the focus and open its Scattering tab: the sphere’s cross-sections by Lorenz–Mie theory, the power it scatters in this focus, and where that light goes. The focus is taken as its full vector field: every sample of the objective’s pupil is a plane wave onto the sphere, and the scattered field is the sum of each wave’s Mie field. In the bench plane the pattern is a dipole’s, with little light along the polarisation, and at this size a little more goes forward than back, which a single dipole (Rayleigh) misses. Set Model to Rayleigh to compare. Collecting NA and Collected from say how much of the scattered light a lens would gather: here NA 0.5 looking back along the beam. Change the Particle to a rod to run a dipole array instead.",
